        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change follows the established pattern well \u2014 a transient household-derived field on `Owner`, stamped by `OwnerHousehold`, read by the mapper as a pure transformation \u2014 and splitting `membershipLevel` into `rawMembershipLevel` (pure formula) + `membershipLevel` (applies the ceiling) is clean and idiomatic. But two structural points are worth addressing:\n\n1. **`maxMemberLevel` is a query method that mutates other owners as a hidden side effect.** Inside its stream it calls `other.setHouseholdSize(sizeIn(other, counts))` for each *other* household member before reading `rawMembershipLevel(other)`. A method named for computing a maximum should not be stamping unrelated entities' `householdSize`. This conflates two responsibilities and makes the correctness of the max depend on a mutation buried in a `mapToInt`.\n\n   The reason it's needed is an ordering fragility: `rawMembershipLevel` \u2192 `membershipPoints` reads `getHouseholdSize()`, but in `stampAll` the single-pass `forEach` hasn't stamped every owner's size yet when the first owner's `maxMemberLevel` runs, so it re-stamps them inline. Restructure `stampAll` into two passes \u2014 stamp all `householdSize` values first, then compute `householdMaxMembershipLevel` in a second pass \u2014 so `maxMemberLevel` becomes a pure read over already-stamped owners and the inline `setHouseholdSize` disappears. `stamp` (single) can stamp its own size, then compute the max reading others' sizes via a helper, keeping the mutation out of the max computation.\n\n2. **Secondary / discretionary: `OwnerHousehold` now depends on `OwnerMapper`.** Reusing `rawMembershipLevel` avoids duplicating the leveling formula, which is the right call over copying it. But it does invert the intended layering (the mapper is documented as the pure downstream transformation, yet the upstream stamper now calls into it). This is acceptable as-is given the formula lives in the mapper, but if the two-pass refactor above is done, consider whether the raw-level formula belongs in a small shared unit both can use rather than the stamper reaching into the mapper. Not blocking on its own."
